\details{
The \code{"manuscript"} style lays out a comparison table as the journal
submissions did: a full grid, a two-row header with the grouping's name
spanning its levels, one overall \code{n} column, shaded section rows that
span the table, a bold header, and no font of its own, so the table
takes the body font of the document it is saved into. It needs a table
from \code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}}, and \code{\link[=hv_man_table_save]{hv_man_table_save()}} requires a \code{caption}
for it. See \code{\link[=hv_table_style]{hv_table_style()}} for the properties of each style.

The rest of this page describes the \code{"report"} style.

You give this a \code{\link[gtsummary:tbl_summary]{gtsummary::tbl_summary()}} (or any gtsummary table object
supporting \code{\link[gtsummary:as_flex_table]{gtsummary::as_flex_table()}}), and you get back a \code{flextable}
that already complies with HVTI CORR's "Table Construction for
Manuscripts" rules: a single, non-merged header row (no spanning parent
cells over grouped columns), no merged row-group section-header cells,
a left-justified label column, and Times New Roman at the house font
size. Section rows are shaded across every cell, unmerged, in the
style's fill (\verb{#CAEDFB} by default), and the header is bold.

\code{gtsummary::as_flex_table()} already emits one header row per group with
self-contained labels (e.g. \code{"B\\nN = 45"}), not a merged spanning header.
Any merged region is undone with \code{\link[flextable:merge_none]{flextable::merge_none()}}. That
satisfies the "format the table as flat as possible ... simple
non-merged column titles" rule.

The sections of an \code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}} table (its \code{groups} names, such
as "Demography") become bold header rows in the label column, each
immediately above its first variable, rather than a column of their
own.

Rounding, \verb{\%}-free percentage cells, and \verb{±}-without-spaces formatting are
yours to control, via the \code{statistic}/\code{digits} arguments to
\code{\link[gtsummary:tbl_summary]{gtsummary::tbl_summary()}}; see the package README for a worked example
using \code{\link[gtsummary:style_sigfig]{gtsummary::style_sigfig()}} for 2-significant-figure rounding.

Submitting to JTCVS instead? Use \code{\link[=hv_man_table_jtcvs]{hv_man_table_jtcvs()}}, which builds the
two-row merged spanning header that journal's template expects: CORR
house style and JTCVS submission format want different things from the
same header row.

A table from \code{\link[=hv_tbl_summary]{hv_tbl_summary()}} works here as well as in the JTCVS
renderer. That function writes each cell as \code{"{N_nonmiss} ||| {stat}"}, and
this one splits the two apart into a flat \code{No.} column immediately
before the statistic it counts. Those are the same two values JTCVS
mode puts under a merged spanning header, without the merge. House
rule 8 wants that non-missing count, and \code{\link[=hv_man_footnotes]{hv_man_footnotes()}}'s \code{*}
footnote describes it, so it is kept rather than discarded. A plain
\code{\link[gtsummary:tbl_summary]{gtsummary::tbl_summary()}} table carries no such convention and passes
through unchanged.
